  "title": "Osteosarcoma in Dogs | Department of Veterinary Medicine",
  "text": "Osteosarcoma in Dogs | Department of Veterinary Medicine\nSkip to main content\nDepartment of Veterinary Medicine\nMenu\nHome\nAbout us\nNews\nResearch\nStudy with us\nStaff Directory\nJob and scholarship opportunities\nIntranet\nVeterinary Services\nMore\nHome\nAbout us\nNews\nResearch\nStudy with us\nStaff Directory\nJob and scholarship opportunities\nIntranet\nVeterinary Services\nAbout us\nAbout us overview\nContact us\nHow to find us\nHistory\nAccreditation\nStrategy\nVetEd 2027\nResearch\nResearch overview\nResearch themes\nResearch news\nClinical trials\nResearch facilities\nFellowship applications\nCambridge Festival\nStudy with us\nStudy with us overview\nVeterinary Medicine\nPostgraduate research\nClinical training scholarships\nExternships\nCambridge VetCAST\nStaff Directory\nStaff Directory overview\nPeople\nResearchers by theme\nIntranet\nIntranet overview\nVet School Information Hub\nMenu\nHome\nAbout us\nNews\nResearch\nStudy with us\nStaff Directory\nJob and scholarship opportunities\nIntranet\nVeterinary Services\nAbout us\nAbout us overview\nContact us\nHow to find us\nHistory\nAccreditation\nStrategy\nVetEd 2027\nResearch\nResearch overview\nResearch themes\nResearch news\nClinical trials\nResearch facilities\nFellowship applications\nCambridge Festival\nClinical trials\nClinical trials overview\nBOAS\nBOAS\nBOAS overview\nAbout BOAS\nOur team\nRespiratory Function Assessment\nBOAS anatomy\nPublications\nInfographics\nMRes Research Project\nStudy with us\nStudy with us overview\nVeterinary Medicine\nPostgraduate research\nClinical training scholarships\nExternships\nCambridge VetCAST\nVeterinary Medicine\nVeterinary Medicine overview\nWhy choose Cambridge?\nOur course\nVet life at Cambridge\nVeterinary Societies\nVet life after Cambridge\nHelp with your finances\nHow to apply\nGraduates, over-21s & ‘transfers’\nStudents from outside the UK\nOpen days and residentials\nFrequently Asked Questions\nPostgraduate research\nPostgraduate research overview\n'NEW' CamGuide for PhDs\nHow to apply\nDegree programmes\nAvailable PhD /MPhil projects\nFunding\nClinical training scholarships\nClinical training scholarships overview\nJunior clinical training scholarships/ Internships\nSenior clinical training scholarships/ Residencies\nJunior clinical training scholarships/ Internships\nJunior clinical training scholarships/ Internships overview\nJCTS Rotating in small animal studies\nSenior clinical training scholarships/ Residencies\nSenior clinical training scholarships/ Residencies overview\nSCTS Anaesthesia\nSCTS Neurology\nSCTS Diagnostic Imaging\nSCTS Small Animal Surgery\nSCTS Clinical Pathology\nStaff Directory\nStaff Directory overview\nPeople\nResearchers by theme\nIntranet\nIntranet overview\nVet School Information Hub\nHome\nOsteosarcoma In Dogs\nOsteosarcoma in Dogs\nCall for Patients\nThe Surgical Discovery Centre and the Oncology Team at the Queens Veterinary School Hospital are currently running a clinical research study on osteosarcoma in dogs.\nOsteosarcoma is an aggressive bone cancer that is seen in humans and in dogs. Dogs are at least 10 times more likely than humans to develop osteosarcoma. This project looks at a new blood test that can identify tumour cells that may be present in the patient’s blood – it is thought that these circulating tumour cells, if present, may be an early indicator of disease progression.  We have received funding from Sarcoma UK to investigate this further. From just a small blood sample we are able to determine whether there are circulating tumour cells in the patient. We plan to monitor blood samples before, during and after treatment to see if there is any connection between levels of circulating cells in blood and disease progression. Since the pattern of disease in osteosarcoma is similar in dogs and in humans, it is hoped that the results of this work will also translate to humans, improving our ability to monitor and manage these patients.\nWe are actively looking for dogs with a suspected or confirmed diagnosis of osteosarcoma for potential inclusion in the study.\nFurther information can be found at:\nwww.sarcoma.org.uk/research/funded-projects/looking-dogs-answers\nPlease contact: Nicola Cumbridge (Clinical Research Co-ordinator)\nclinical-research@vet\n.cam.ac.uk\nfor further details.\nDepartment of Veterinary Medicine\nContact Information\nMadingley Road\nCambridge\nCB3 0ES\nenquiries@vet.cam.ac.uk\n+44 (0)1223 337701\nUniversity of Cambridge\n© 2026 University of Cambridge\nCookies\nPrivacy policy\nAccessibility\nFreedom of information\nModern slavery\nTerms and conditions",
